100
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:15,000
In the operation of plutonium-bearing fuels, several reactions take place. When a neutron

101
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:25,000
is captured in plutonium-239, it yields plutonium-240, a fertile material. Subsequent capture yields

102
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:35,000
an excellent fuel. If a neutron is captured in 241, it yields plutonium-242.

103
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Experiments are underway to determine the rate of these reactions and their accumulative effect

104
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:53,000
on reactivity. Test elements have been operated to failure to determine ultimate limits.

105
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:02,000
And for the assurance of safety under undesirable conditions, deliberately defective elements are irradiated.

106
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:14,000
Ceramic fuels have performed very well at power generation rates more than four times those utilized in contemporary water-cooled power reactors.

107
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:25,000
Allied investigations ascertain void migration, fuel relocation, and the extent of the molten

108
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:36,000
